---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const description =
  "Stamp writes a disk image to a USB stick. The whole stick is erased. Internal disks are not listed.";
---

<Base title="Stamp" description={description}>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="seal" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 200 200">
        <circle cx="100" cy="100" r="94" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" />
        <circle cx="100" cy="100" r="84" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" />
        <text
          x="100"
          y="108"
          text-anchor="middle"
          fill="currentColor"
          font-size="32"
          font-family="Newsreader, serif"
          font-weight="500">STAMP</text>
        <text
          x="100"
          y="134"
          text-anchor="middle"
          fill="currentColor"
          font-size="13"
          letter-spacing="3.5"
          font-family="Source Sans 3, sans-serif">USB</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Quickshell, for Omarchy</p>
      <h1>Stamp</h1>
      <p class="lede">{description}</p>
      <nav>
        <a href="#built">How it's built</a>
        <a href="#download">Download</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/varrtto/stamp">Source</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section id="use">
    <h2>What you do</h2>
    <p>
      Pick a disk image, pick a USB stick, and type that stick’s name. Stamp
      then asks for authorization and copies the image over the whole device.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <span>01</span>
        <span>Choose an <code>.iso</code>, an <code>.img</code>, or any other image file.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>02</span>
        <span>Choose a USB stick. Only whole USB disks are offered.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>03</span>
        <span>Type the kernel name, such as <code>sdb</code>, to confirm the erase.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>04</span>
        <span>Approve the system authorization dialog. The window itself never runs as root.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>05</span>
        <span>Leave the stick plugged in through the sync. Unplug it when Stamp says you can.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="built">
    <h2>How it's built</h2>
    <p>
      Stamp is a Quickshell window and a shell script, kept apart so the
      interface can stay unprivileged. The colors on this page are Stamp’s own
      defaults. On Omarchy, the window reads your current theme and uses those
      colors instead.
    </p>

    <h3>The window</h3>
    <p>
      <code>shell.qml</code> is a Qt Quick window run by <code>qs</code>. Every
      few seconds it asks <code>lsblk</code> for block devices and keeps disks
      whose transport is USB. Read-only disks are dropped, and so is anything
      mounted at <code>/</code>, <code>/boot</code>, <code>/efi</code>, or
      <code>/home</code>. The file picker records the image path; <code>stat</code>
      and <code>file</code> supply the size and a short description.
    </p>
    <p>
      The confirmation field has to match the device name before anything else
      happens. Stamp then runs <code>flash.sh --check</code>. That pass only
      reports whether the image fits. The write starts after the check, through
      <code>pkexec</code>, which is the normal polkit prompt.
    </p>

    <h3>The writer</h3>
    <p>
      <code>flash.sh</code> is the only part that opens the disk. It checks the
      same facts again, as root, and stops if any of them fail.
    </p>
    <ul class="checks">
      <li>The path is a whole disk, <code>/dev/sdX</code> or <code>/dev/nvmeXnY</code>, not a partition and not a symlink.</li>
      <li><code>lsblk</code> reports type <code>disk</code>, transport <code>usb</code>, and a writable device.</li>
      <li>The disk is not the one that holds <code>/</code>, <code>/boot</code>, <code>/efi</code>, or <code>/home</code>.</li>
      <li>The image is a regular file outside <code>/dev</code>, <code>/sys</code>, and <code>/proc</code>, and it is smaller than the stick.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When the checks pass, the script unmounts filesystems on the stick, turns
      off swap that points at it, and copies the image with <code>dd</code> in
      4&nbsp;MiB blocks. <code>dd</code> prints progress on one updating line.
      The script turns those updates into plain lines the window can show:
      percent, bytes, speed, and time left.
    </p>
    <p>
      After the copy, it runs <code>sync</code> and flushes the device. If you
      left “Power off the stick when the write finishes” on, it ejects the
      stick, and uses <code>udisksctl</code> if eject needs another try. Cancel
      is a flag file the script reads between steps. Once sync has started, the
      window stops offering cancel, because the bytes are already on the stick
      and the flush still has to finish.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="download">
    <h2>Download</h2>
    <p>
      Stamp needs Quickshell, which Omarchy already includes, and polkit so the
      write can ask for authorization. Version 0.1.0 is tagged on
      <a href="https://github.com/varrtto/stamp">GitHub</a>. The license is MIT.
    </p>

    <h3>Run it from the source</h3>
    <p>This opens the window from the directory you just cloned.</p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/varrtto/stamp.git
cd stamp
qs -p .</code></pre>

    <h3>Install the Arch package</h3>
    <p>
      The repository includes a <code>PKGBUILD</code>. Building it installs the
      <code>stamp-usb</code> command and a Stamp entry in the application menu.
      The package depends on <code>quickshell</code>, <code>polkit</code>, and
      <code>file</code>. <code>udisks2</code> is optional and covers the power-off fallback.
    </p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/varrtto/stamp.git
cd stamp/packaging
makepkg -si</code></pre>
    <p class="note">
      <code>makepkg</code> downloads the v0.1.0 source archive from GitHub and
      checks its hash before installing.
    </p>

    <h3>Install the Debian package</h3>
    <p>
      Each update of this page includes
      <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}stamp-usb.deb`}>stamp-usb.deb</a>,
      built for Debian and Ubuntu. It installs the <code>stamp-usb</code> command
      and a Stamp entry in the application menu.
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo apt install ./stamp-usb.deb</code></pre>
    <p class="note">
      The package needs <code>quickshell</code>, which Debian testing and
      unstable provide, along with Ubuntu 26.10. On an older Ubuntu release,
      install Quickshell from the DankLinux PPA first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="footer">
    <p>
      Stamp is by Alfredo Gore.{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/varrtto/stamp">github.com/varrtto/stamp</a>
    </p>
  </footer>
</Base>
